            [
                'title' => 'Chest pain at night: when to wait and when to call an ambulance',
                'category' => 'health-tips', 'author' => 'Prof. Dr. Ashraful Haque', 'read_minutes' => 6,
                'is_featured' => true,
                'excerpt' => 'Most chest pain is not a heart attack. But a few specific features change the calculation entirely, and they are worth knowing before you are the one deciding at 3am.',
                'body' => "Most chest pain that arrives in our emergency department turns out not to be cardiac. Acid reflux, muscular strain and anxiety account for a large share of presentations. That is genuinely reassuring — but it is also why people talk themselves out of coming in when they should not.\n\n## The features that should not wait\n\nCall an ambulance, rather than driving yourself, if chest discomfort comes with any of the following:\n\n- Pressure, heaviness or tightness rather than a sharp stab, lasting more than a few minutes\n- Pain spreading to the jaw, neck, left arm or between the shoulder blades\n- Sweating that appears alongside the pain, particularly cold sweat\n- Breathlessness out of proportion to what you were doing\n- Nausea or vomiting together with the discomfort\n- A sense of impending doom — patients describe this often, and it deserves to be taken seriously\n\n## Why the ambulance rather than a car\n\nThis is the part people get wrong most often. An advanced life-support ambulance carries a defibrillator and a paramedic who can record an ECG and transmit it ahead. If the rhythm deteriorates in a private car in Dhaka traffic, nothing can be done. If it deteriorates in an ambulance, it can be shocked within seconds.\n\nIt also means the receiving team knows what is coming. When our emergency department gets an ECG before the patient arrives, the catheterisation laboratory can be activated in advance, which removes twenty to thirty minutes from the treatment time.\n\n## Women, and why the pattern is often different\n\nWomen having heart attacks are more likely to present without the textbook crushing central chest pain. Fatigue that is out of character, breathlessness, nausea and upper back discomfort can all be the dominant symptom. This is a substantial reason why women are diagnosed later and treated later. If something feels significantly wrong and you cannot explain it, get it checked.\n\n## What happens when you arrive\n\nYou will be triaged within minutes and have an ECG almost immediately — this is not a queue you wait in. If the ECG shows a blocked artery, you go directly to the catheterisation laboratory. If it does not, blood tests for cardiac enzymes are taken and repeated a few hours later, because early tests can be normal in a heart attack that is still evolving.\n\nBeing sent home after investigation is a good outcome, not a wasted trip.",
