            [
                'title' => 'Dengue season: the warning signs that mean go to hospital now',
                'category' => 'health-tips', 'author' => 'Dr. Naeem Uddin Bhuiyan', 'read_minutes' => 5,
                'is_featured' => true,
                'excerpt' => 'The dangerous phase of dengue often begins as the fever comes down, not while it is at its peak. Here is what to watch for.',
                'body' => "Dengue follows a pattern that catches families out every monsoon, because the most dangerous period does not look like the most dangerous period.\n\n## The three phases\n\n**Febrile phase (days 1–3).** High fever, severe headache, pain behind the eyes, muscle and joint aches. Unpleasant, but usually not the risky part.\n\n**Critical phase (days 4–6).** The fever settles. Many families relax here — and this is precisely when plasma leakage can begin. Most severe dengue complications occur in this window.\n\n**Recovery phase (day 7 onwards).** Appetite returns and a rash may appear as things improve.\n\n## Warning signs that need hospital assessment\n\nGo to a hospital immediately if any of these appear, particularly as the fever falls:\n\n- Severe or persistent abdominal pain\n- Persistent vomiting — more than three times in an hour\n- Bleeding from gums or nose, or blood in vomit or stool\n- Black, tarry stools\n- Lethargy, restlessness or unusual irritability\n- Cold, clammy hands and feet\n- Passing much less urine than usual\n- Difficulty breathing\n\n## Managing the mild case at home\n\nParacetamol is the only fever medicine that is safe. Do not take ibuprofen, aspirin, diclofenac or any similar anti-inflammatory — they increase bleeding risk in dengue and we see avoidable complications from this every season.\n\nFluid intake matters more than any medication. Oral saline, coconut water, rice water, soup and plain water are all appropriate. Aim for pale urine passed at least every four to six hours.\n\nHave a platelet count and haematocrit checked daily from day three, and keep the results so trends are visible. A single value tells you far less than a direction of travel.\n\n## Preventing it in the first place\n\nAedes mosquitoes breed in clean, still water and bite during the day. Empty and scrub anything holding water — flower pot trays, buckets, discarded containers, air-conditioner drip trays, water tank lids — twice a week. The scrubbing matters, because eggs stick to the sides and survive drying.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'RBR Hospital opens a second cardiac catheterisation laboratory',
                'category' => 'news', 'author' => 'RBR Hospital', 'read_minutes' => 3,
                'excerpt' => 'The new lab doubles capacity for emergency angioplasty and removes the waiting time that previously built up when both an emergency and a planned case arrived together.',
                'body' => "RBR Hospital has commissioned a second cardiac catheterisation laboratory, doubling capacity for both emergency and planned coronary intervention.\n\nThe practical effect is on emergency care. Previously, a single laboratory meant that a heart attack arriving during a planned procedure had to wait for the room to clear. With two laboratories, one can be kept available for primary angioplasty at all times.\n\n\"Door-to-balloon time is the single strongest predictor of how much heart muscle a patient keeps,\" said Professor Dr. Ashraful Haque, Head of Interventional Cardiology. \"Our median has been under sixty minutes. With the second laboratory we expect to bring that down further, and more importantly to remove the occasional long outlier when two emergencies arrived together.\"\n\nThe new laboratory is equipped with flat-panel digital imaging with reduced radiation dose, intravascular ultrasound and fractional flow reserve measurement for assessing borderline narrowings, and a rotational atherectomy system for heavily calcified arteries.\n\nThe unit is operational now and accepts emergency referrals from other hospitals through the emergency hotline on 10666.",
