            [
                'title' => 'Five numbers every adult over 35 should know about themselves',
                'category' => 'health-tips', 'author' => 'Prof. Dr. Abdul Matin Sarker', 'read_minutes' => 5,
                'excerpt' => 'Blood pressure, HbA1c, LDL cholesterol, waist circumference and eGFR. Between them, they predict most of what will go wrong, and all five are cheap to measure.',
                'body' => "Preventive medicine is unglamorous. But a small number of measurements predict a large share of what actually shortens life in Bangladesh, and none of them is expensive to check.\n\n## 1. Blood pressure\n\n**Target: under 130/80 mmHg for most adults.**\n\nHigh blood pressure causes no symptoms until it causes a stroke. It is the leading modifiable risk factor for stroke, and stroke is one of the leading causes of death and disability here. Have it checked at least annually — and if it is raised once, have it rechecked properly rather than dismissed.\n\n## 2. HbA1c\n\n**Target: under 5.7% is normal; 5.7–6.4% is prediabetes; 6.5% or above indicates diabetes.**\n\nHbA1c reflects the average glucose over roughly three months, so it cannot be gamed by fasting the night before. Bangladesh has a high and rising prevalence of type 2 diabetes, and a substantial proportion of cases are undiagnosed. Prediabetes is reversible with weight loss and activity — that window is worth catching.\n\n## 3. LDL cholesterol\n\n**Target: under 100 mg/dL for most; under 70 mg/dL if you already have heart disease or diabetes.**\n\nLDL is the fraction that drives arterial plaque. South Asians tend to develop coronary disease earlier and at lower body weights than European populations, so a normal BMI is not reassurance on its own.\n\n## 4. Waist circumference\n\n**Target: under 90 cm for men, under 80 cm for women.**\n\nFor South Asian populations, waist measurement predicts metabolic risk better than BMI does, because the fat distribution differs. A tape measure costs almost nothing and tells you something a weighing scale does not.\n\n## 5. eGFR\n\n**Target: above 60 mL/min/1.73m².**\n\nEstimated glomerular filtration rate, calculated from serum creatinine, reflects kidney function. Chronic kidney disease is silent until it is advanced, and diabetes and hypertension are the two leading causes — which is exactly why the first four numbers matter for this one.\n\n## What to do with them\n\nWrite them down with the date, and keep the list. A single reading is a snapshot; five years of readings is a trajectory, and a trajectory is what lets a physician act early rather than react late.",
