            [
                'title' => 'Newborn hearing screening is now offered to every baby born here',
                'category' => 'news', 'author' => 'RBR Hospital', 'read_minutes' => 3,
                'excerpt' => 'Congenital hearing loss affects a small but meaningful number of newborns, and detecting it in the first months changes language outcomes substantially.',
                'body' => "Every baby delivered at RBR Hospital is now offered hearing screening before discharge, as a routine part of newborn care rather than a test parents have to know to ask for.\n\nThe screening uses otoacoustic emission testing, which is painless, takes a few minutes, and is usually done while the baby sleeps. Babies who do not pass the initial screen are recalled for a fuller assessment; most turn out to have normal hearing, with the initial result explained by fluid or debris in the ear canal.\n\nThe rationale is timing. Language development depends on hearing during the first months and years of life. Without screening, congenital hearing loss is often not identified until a child is two or three years old and not speaking — by which point a significant developmental window has closed. Detected in the first months, hearing aids or cochlear implantation can be arranged early enough that language development proceeds close to normally.\n\n\"The test is quick and cheap, and the consequence of missing it is measured in years of a child's development,\" said Dr. Mizanur Rahman Khan, Head of ENT. \"There was no good argument for making it optional.\"\n\nThe service is included in the maternity package at no additional charge. Babies born elsewhere can be booked for screening through the ENT department.",
