            [
                'title' => 'Before you take that antibiotic: four questions worth asking',
                'category' => 'health-tips', 'author' => 'Dr. Naeem Uddin Bhuiyan', 'read_minutes' => 4,
                'excerpt' => 'Antibiotic resistance in Bangladesh is now high enough that some routine infections are difficult to treat. Much of the driver is avoidable.',
                'body' => "Antibiotics are among the most valuable medicines ever developed, and we are steadily using them into ineffectiveness. Resistance rates for several common organisms in Bangladesh are now high enough that infections which were once straightforward require second- or third-line drugs.\n\nMost of the driver is not exotic. It is antibiotics taken for viral illness, bought without prescription, and stopped early.\n\n## Four questions worth asking\n\n**1. Is this infection actually bacterial?**\n\nMost sore throats, colds, coughs and cases of flu are viral. Antibiotics do nothing for them — no faster recovery, no reduced complications — while still contributing to resistance and side effects. Green nasal discharge is not evidence of bacterial infection; it is normal in the course of a viral cold.\n\n**2. Is this the narrowest drug that will work?**\n\nBroad-spectrum antibiotics kill a wider range of organisms, including the useful ones in your gut, and select more strongly for resistance. Where a narrow-spectrum drug will treat the likely organism, it is the better choice.\n\n**3. Should we culture first?**\n\nFor urinary infections, wound infections and recurrent problems, a culture and sensitivity test identifies the actual organism and which drugs it responds to. It takes a day or two, but it replaces guessing with knowing — and for recurrent infection, guessing has usually already failed.\n\n**4. How long, exactly?**\n\nCourse lengths have shortened for many infections as evidence has accumulated. Ask for the specific number of days and complete it. Stopping when you feel better leaves the hardiest organisms behind, which is precisely the population you do not want surviving.\n\n## What not to do\n\nDo not buy antibiotics without a prescription. Do not use leftover antibiotics from a previous illness. Do not take someone else's. Do not press a doctor for an antibiotic for a viral illness — and if a doctor explains why one is not needed, that is good medicine, not indifference.",
